1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,166
This is a short look at one thing: a proof of delivery,

2
00:00:04,166 --> 00:00:06,178
from the cab to the invoice.

3
00:00:06,178 --> 00:00:09,290
All jobs and times shown are illustrative.

4
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:10,575
Monday afternoon,

5
00:00:10,575 --> 00:00:13,764
a job is booked against your customer's reference:

6
00:00:13,764 --> 00:00:17,035
26 pallets from Zaltbommel to the Milan area.

7
00:00:17,035 --> 00:00:19,397
It goes to the driver's phone with the trip,

8
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:22,412
so the paperwork starts where the cargo does.

9
00:00:22,612 --> 00:00:27,784
Tuesday, 14:12. Your driver delivers near Milan.

10
00:00:27,784 --> 00:00:31,064
On the phone the driver takes the signature and a photo of the

11
00:00:31,064 --> 00:00:31,729
load.

12
00:00:31,729 --> 00:00:35,189
Time and place are stamped, and the POD sits on the job,

13
00:00:35,189 --> 00:00:37,457
not in a folder in the cab.

14
00:00:37,657 --> 00:00:41,094
With the POD on the job, the job closes.

15
00:00:41,094 --> 00:00:44,784
The invoice is raised the same afternoon, in your own system,

16
00:00:44,784 --> 00:00:47,241
against your customer's reference.

17
00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:50,804
Delivered at 14:12, invoiced at 14:20.

18
00:00:50,804 --> 00:00:54,486
The job bills on the day it closed.

19
00:00:54,486 --> 00:00:56,715
When jobs bill on the day they close,

20
00:00:56,715 --> 00:01:00,027
the money starts moving a day or two earlier.

21
00:01:00,227 --> 00:01:02,372
Now the one that matters.

22
00:01:02,772 --> 00:01:06,604
Wednesday, a next-day delivery in Belgium.

23
00:01:06,604 --> 00:01:11,383
The status says delivered. No POD has come back.

24
00:01:11,383 --> 00:01:14,460
The job stays open, and a counter starts.

25
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:18,118
On Thursday morning it is at the top of the outstanding list,

26
00:01:18,118 --> 00:01:19,297
oldest first.

27
00:01:19,297 --> 00:01:21,491
Your office does not chase it.

28
00:01:21,491 --> 00:01:23,942
An Axe Global specialist calls the driver,

29
00:01:23,942 --> 00:01:25,634
then the receiver's goods-in,

30
00:01:25,634 --> 00:01:28,193
gets the signed copy and attaches it.

31
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:30,721
The invoice goes out before midday.

32
00:01:30,721 --> 00:01:33,027
Had the receiver not answered by Friday,

33
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:36,371
the job would turn red and reach your team with every call

34
00:01:36,371 --> 00:01:37,636
already logged.

35
00:01:37,636 --> 00:01:42,211
The system finds the gap. A person closes it.

36
00:01:42,411 --> 00:01:43,606
One exception.

37
00:01:43,606 --> 00:01:48,213
At a Rotterdam terminal the driver ticks waiting time:

38
00:01:48,213 --> 00:01:51,002
95 minutes on site, 60 free.

39
00:01:51,002 --> 00:01:55,149
The extra 35 minutes go onto a supplemental invoice with the

40
00:01:55,149 --> 00:01:56,862
POD times attached.

41
00:01:56,862 --> 00:01:59,323
Billed, not written off at month end.

42
00:02:00,123 --> 00:02:10,404
Which leaves what we do not know yet: today, do your PODs reach you on paper, by photo or through a driver app, and how long does a job sit before it is invoiced?

